  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "For the function $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)={x}^{2},$ which of the following  statements are true? \n\n- The function is an injection\n- The function is a surjection\n- The function is a bijection",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $\u2200\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $\u2200\\,y\\in Y,$ $\u2203\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. For instance, $f(2)=4=f(-2)$, but $2\\ne -2.$ So, $f(x)$ is not an injection.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, there is no $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=-1.$ So, $f(x)$ is not a surjection.\n\n- Statement III is false because the function is not both an injection and a surjection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"",
